            <ArticleTitle>Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>205-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.psym.2013.01.014</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0033-3182(13)00015-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The presentation of posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) features neuropsychiatric symptoms in the context of predominantly white matter cerebral edema in the setting of a diverse variety of underlying clinical entities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To illustrate the presentation and diagnostic strategy for this under-recognized condition.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">We present two cases of PRES and review the available literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">PRES may be due to a number of underlying conditions, but typically presents with symptoms consistent with delirium.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Psychiatrist practicing in the general hospital should be aware of the presentation and appropriate work-up of PRES to forestall serious potential sequelae.</AbstractText>
